WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.532
[MUSIC]

00:00:07.532 --> 00:00:12.164
For as long as I can remember, or
at least since I was four years old,

00:00:12.164 --> 00:00:14.200
I wanted to start a company.

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:20.730
By the age of eight, I had launched
my first somewhat successful venture.

00:00:20.730 --> 00:00:24.100
Which involved reselling
my mom's Evian bottles

00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:26.715
from her fridge to construction
workers in our neighborhood.

00:00:26.715 --> 00:00:30.762
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; She purchased these at around $1.50

00:00:30.762 --> 00:00:35.085
a bottle, and I resold them for
$.50, and they sold out great.

00:00:35.085 --> 00:00:36.853
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:00:36.853 --> 00:00:42.000
&gt;&gt; But, this was an early lesson that
even when your mom is an investor,

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:44.730
it's important to generate
a positive return on capital for

00:00:44.730 --> 00:00:46.065
the business to be sustainable.

00:00:46.065 --> 00:00:51.020
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Fast forward 20 years, and

00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:55.500
I got that call,
the call that we all know well,

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:58.600
that I had gotten into
Stanford Graduate School of Business.

00:01:00.010 --> 00:01:02.460
It feels a lot like winning the lottery.

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:08.740
We all know the stats,
less than 7% acceptance rate.

00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:13.010
Which means that a lot of very
qualified people who deserve to be here

00:01:13.010 --> 00:01:17.930
don't get the chance to, so
I felt pretty blessed, and

00:01:17.930 --> 00:01:20.469
I was very excited to be starting
my entrepreneurial journey.

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:27.503
That is until few weeks later I came
across another statistic, that somewhere

00:01:27.503 --> 00:01:32.904
between 1% and 5% of venture capital
funding goes to women entrepreneurs.

00:01:32.904 --> 00:01:36.390
Said another way,

00:01:36.390 --> 00:01:41.740
it is more likely to get into
Stanford Business School

00:01:41.740 --> 00:01:45.260
than it is to be a woman entrepreneur
who secures venture capital funding.

00:01:46.690 --> 00:01:47.970
I found this to be shocking.

00:01:49.070 --> 00:01:52.410
And as someone who came from
a background in investing and

00:01:52.410 --> 00:01:55.730
fundraising, I thought,
this is a great business idea.

00:01:57.600 --> 00:01:59.070
It must be.

00:01:59.070 --> 00:02:03.000
There must be a market deficiency
that I could solve through a company.

00:02:05.570 --> 00:02:08.560
It's even the case with the popular show,
Shark Tank.

00:02:10.670 --> 00:02:15.160
Women who pitch themselves for Shark Tank
are less likely to receive capital.

00:02:16.220 --> 00:02:17.590
And when they do receive capital,

00:02:17.590 --> 00:02:21.610
they're more likely to be priced down by
the Sharks than their male counterparts.

00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:25.150
So back to my business.

00:02:26.450 --> 00:02:28.780
If there's anything I've
learned as an investor,

00:02:28.780 --> 00:02:33.150
it's that being right on the wrong time
frame is the same thing as being wrong.

00:02:34.610 --> 00:02:37.540
And turns out,
I was a little bit late to the game.

00:02:37.540 --> 00:02:41.680
There's actually plenty of companies
out there that are doing this today.

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:46.330
Serving women entrepreneurs through
are alternative capital markets,

00:02:46.330 --> 00:02:50.830
through accelerators, through
networks of professional women, but

00:02:50.830 --> 00:02:54.058
you don't hear about this
in the narrative today.

00:02:55.869 --> 00:03:00.839
And so I'm asking myself why, when there
are so many opportunities for women

00:03:00.839 --> 00:03:05.980
entrepreneurs, is the only thing that
you hear about, is how difficult it is?

00:03:05.980 --> 00:03:11.020
I'd like to propose that we
need to reframe the discussion.

00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:14.423
Because actually there's never been
a better time to be a woman entrepreneur.

00:03:17.313 --> 00:03:22.130
Some venture capital firms do
recognize this opportunity.

00:03:22.130 --> 00:03:27.330
First Round Capital, in a study it's
ten years of operating history,

00:03:27.330 --> 00:03:33.040
found that women lead ventures
outperform their male only firms by 63%.

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:38.470
But whether you'e a man or
woman, as an entrepreneur,

00:03:38.470 --> 00:03:43.250
it's important to recognize that venture
capital isn't one size fits all.

00:03:43.250 --> 00:03:45.150
It's not the right solution for
every business.

00:03:46.830 --> 00:03:48.830
Questions that you
should ask yourself are,

00:03:48.830 --> 00:03:51.200
how important is it to me to
retain control of my company?

00:03:52.510 --> 00:03:54.380
How quickly do I want to
grow this business?

00:03:54.380 --> 00:03:58.090
If you decide that venture
capital is right for

00:03:58.090 --> 00:04:03.490
you, it's also important to note that not
all venture firms are created equally.

00:04:03.490 --> 00:04:08.295
Some specialize by industry, others
focus on later stage versus early stage.

00:04:08.295 --> 00:04:13.600
GSB alumna, Jessica Herron,
who's the founder of stella and

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:18.424
dot, recognized the importance of finding
the right capital for her business.

00:04:18.424 --> 00:04:23.160
She shopped around her idea for
a direct to consumer jewelry business

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:27.210
to many different investors before
finding the right one who believed,

00:04:27.210 --> 00:04:31.410
not only in her business model, but
also the vision that she had for

00:04:31.410 --> 00:04:33.940
how quickly she was going to get there.

00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:37.930
It was important to her to prioritize, not
only her business, but also her family.

00:04:38.950 --> 00:04:43.590
And arguably, her wildly successful
business is well positioned today because

00:04:43.590 --> 00:04:46.450
of the time that she took to
find the right capital provider.

00:04:49.110 --> 00:04:51.850
Outside of venture capital there are so

00:04:51.850 --> 00:04:55.680
many resources today, in the form
of crowdfunding and equity funding.

00:04:56.710 --> 00:04:59.940
And the great news is that women
are disproportionately successful in

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:00.469
these channels.

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:06.135
Kickstarter reports that women lead
tech businesses are more than twice as

00:05:06.135 --> 00:05:09.587
likely to be successful in
raising campaigns than men.

00:05:11.707 --> 00:05:16.252
Another GSB Alumna, Neja Woodward,
is using Kickstarter to prove

00:05:16.252 --> 00:05:21.190
early stage product market fit and
gain customer traction.

00:05:21.190 --> 00:05:23.540
She's designing a line of dolls and

00:05:23.540 --> 00:05:27.400
stories that are inspiring
the next generation of girls,

00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:32.850
that they can be women entrepreneurs,
or coders, journalists, or politicians.

00:05:32.850 --> 00:05:35.315
And, she's doing so through Kickstarter.

00:05:37.900 --> 00:05:40.530
So, the way that Kickstarter and
crowd funding works,

00:05:40.530 --> 00:05:46.390
is that early contributors give capital
in exchange for goods or services.

00:05:48.050 --> 00:05:53.000
Outside of traditional crowd funding,
there is equity crowd funding,

00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:56.710
in which entrepreneurs can
actually raise real equity.

00:05:58.570 --> 00:06:01.230
And women are also really kicking
ass in this channel as well.

00:06:03.030 --> 00:06:05.590
CircleUp, which is one of
the leading firms in this space,

00:06:05.590 --> 00:06:11.040
reports that women are more than five
times as successful raising capital

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:14.470
online than they are through VC.

00:06:14.470 --> 00:06:17.860
And more than nine times
more likely to raise capital

00:06:17.860 --> 00:06:21.300
online than they are through
traditional banks.

00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:23.849
One woman entrepreneur who
raised capital through CircleUp

00:06:25.250 --> 00:06:30.580
suggests that raising capital online takes
gender completely out of the equation,

00:06:30.580 --> 00:06:32.350
because most of
the communication is written.

00:06:34.310 --> 00:06:38.115
I'm optimistic about the future for
women entrepreneurs,

00:06:38.115 --> 00:06:42.665
particularly because some suggest
that alternative capital markets,

00:06:42.665 --> 00:06:47.829
like CircleUp, are going to outstrip
venture capital within the next ten years.

00:06:50.353 --> 00:06:53.006
But as optimistic as I
am about the future,

00:06:53.006 --> 00:06:56.410
it's important to reflect
back on where we've been.

00:06:58.190 --> 00:07:04.480
I was born in January 1988,
into a world in which women

00:07:04.480 --> 00:07:09.770
couldn't secure a business
loan without a male co-signer.

00:07:09.770 --> 00:07:13.340
Within my lifetime women
gained access to this

00:07:13.340 --> 00:07:15.190
fundamental source of business financing.

00:07:17.150 --> 00:07:18.650
That's pretty staggering to me.

00:07:20.610 --> 00:07:24.420
And yet women have been creating
businesses for generations, and

00:07:24.420 --> 00:07:25.390
successfully doing it.

00:07:26.890 --> 00:07:32.360
One example that's close to my heart
is my great grandmother, Myrtle.

00:07:32.360 --> 00:07:36.310
She started a successful fine
fashion business in Muncie, Indiana,

00:07:36.310 --> 00:07:44.350
of all places, in 1956,
when most women were simply housewives.

00:07:44.350 --> 00:07:46.020
And she did so while raising two kids.

00:07:48.580 --> 00:07:53.950
She and the other women who've
come before me are my inspiration.

00:07:56.360 --> 00:08:00.810
I hope that this is just the beginning
of the conversation, and

00:08:00.810 --> 00:08:06.580
that by sharing stories of success,
we can start

00:08:06.580 --> 00:08:11.870
to change the pattern, because venture
capital is all about pattern recognition.

00:08:13.360 --> 00:08:21.580
The more successful women entrepreneurs
that do well with institutional capital,

00:08:21.580 --> 00:08:26.799
the more likely investors
will be to follow the herd.

00:08:26.799 --> 00:08:33.858
As an aspiring woman entrepreneur myself,
I'm throwing my hat in the ring.

00:08:33.858 --> 00:08:36.064
Will you join me?

00:08:36.064 --> 00:08:36.993
Thank you.

00:08:36.993 --> 00:08:39.788
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:08:39.788 --> 00:08:44.769
[MUSIC]

